<!--
  Same house style as the phone module: plain words, short sentences, no em dashes,
  no semicolons, UK spelling. Everything the watch shows lives here so it can be
  translated without touching code.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="app_name">SajdaTime</string>
    <string name="tile_label">Next prayer</string>
    <string name="tile_description">The next prayer and how long you have</string>
    <string name="tile_needs_setup">Set up on your phone</string>

    <string name="wear_locating">Finding your location</string>
    <string name="wear_no_location_title">No location yet</string>
    <string name="wear_no_location_body">Open SajdaTime on your phone, or use Makkah for now.</string>
    <string name="wear_use_makkah">Use Makkah</string>

    <!--
      The watch had no disclaimer at all, so a user who only ever glanced at their wrist
      never saw one. Shortened to fit the screen, saying the same thing as the phone.
      Do not remove it.

      Two of the phone disclaimer's four points survive the shortening: these are a
      calculation, and the mosque wins a disagreement. The other two — how the calculation
      can fail, and that the app carries no warranty — are on the phone, which every watch
      owner also has. See docs/HANDOVER.md §5.15.

      ⚠️ THREE LINES. That is a hard budget, not a style note, and it is why this reads more
      bluntly than the phone's version.

      This is the second-to-last item in the times list, and the list anchors from the
      bottom (see roundListPadding in WearApp.kt), so the top of this block is what rises
      into the narrow cap of the circle — where AppScaffold paints the watch face clock.
      At three lines it stops just clear of it. At four the first line is printed straight
      through by the time, in grey on grey, and the one screen element that must never be
      buried is the one that gets buried.

      Measured on the 192dp round, font scale 1.0, at the bottom of the scroll. Character
      count is a poor guide because the wrap is by word: 88 chars fitted three lines, 85
      needed four, 78 fitted. If you reword this, screenshot the bottom of the scroll —
      `./tools/wear-verify.sh` captures exactly that, on both round sizes and both font
      scales. Do not merge a reworded version you have only read.
    -->
    <string name="wear_disclaimer">A helper, not a religious authority. Times are calculated. Follow your mosque.</string>

    <!--
      Shown only when the day's times were projected from another latitude, which above the
      polar circles is every day for months. %1$d is that latitude — it is not a constant,
      it follows the calculation method, which is why the number is printed rather than
      described. See PrayerEngine.polarReferenceLatitude.

      This exists because the watch was the one surface that said nothing. DayPrayerTimes'
      own contract is "the UI must say so when this is set: an approximation the user is not
      told about is worse than no times at all", and the phone honours it with a banner, the
      PDF with a footnote, the alert with a header, and the tile by printing "About 5:14" —
      while the watch *app* printed six times that looked measured. Three surfaces telling
      the truth and one not is worse than none doing it, because the quiet one is the one
      on the wrist.

      Deliberately not the phone's paragraph. There is no room, and the phone is where the
      full explanation belongs; this says enough to stop the times being trusted as exact
      and points at the person who can settle it.

      It sits ABOVE the times, not down beside the disclaimer. That was tried first and
      screenshotted: after the times it is off the screen entirely at the scroll extreme,
      and on the way there it passes under the watch face clock AppScaffold paints in the
      cap of the circle — so the one line saying "do not trust these to the minute" was the
      one line the user had to hunt for. It costs two prayer rows off the first screen, and
      only for the users who ever see it.
    -->
    <string name="wear_polar_notice">Approximate. Here the sun does not rise or set today, so these come from %1$d°. Ask your mosque.</string>

    <!--
      School of thought, on the wrist.

      The watch is standalone, so it cannot assume the phone app exists or that its
      settings ever arrived. Its own default is Sunni and Shafi'i, which puts Asr out by
      more than an hour for a Hanafi user. The row at the bottom of the times list always
      shows which school produced the times on screen, so a wrong one is visible rather
      than silent, and can be corrected without the phone.

      Translators: these are the same terms as the phone app. Keep them identical, and
      leave the school names as proper nouns rather than translating them.
    -->
    <string name="wear_school">School</string>
    <string name="wear_school_pair">%1$s · %2$s</string>
    <string name="wear_sect_sunni">Sunni</string>
    <string name="wear_sect_shia">Shia</string>
    <string name="wear_madhab_hanafi">Hanafi</string>
    <string name="wear_madhab_shafii">Shafi\'i</string>
    <string name="wear_madhab_maliki">Maliki</string>
    <string name="wear_madhab_hanbali">Hanbali</string>
    <string name="wear_madhab_note">Only Asr changes. Hanafi is later.</string>

    <string name="wear_qibla_needs_location">Set your location to find the Qibla</string>
    <string name="wear_qibla_facing">Facing the Kaaba</string>
    <string name="wear_qibla_bearing">%1$d°</string>
    <string name="wear_qibla_from_true_north">from true north</string>

    <!-- Countdowns. Kept short: a tile line has room for about eight characters. -->
    <string name="wear_duration_h_m">%1$dh %2$dm</string>
    <string name="wear_duration_m">%1$dm</string>
    <string name="wear_in_h_m">in %1$dh %2$dm</string>
    <string name="wear_in_m">in %1$dm</string>
    <!-- Time and countdown on one tile line, e.g. "05:14  ·  in 2h 10m". -->
    <string name="wear_tile_detail">%1$s  ·  %2$s</string>
    <!-- Same line, for a time projected from another latitude above the polar circles.
         "About" rather than a tilde: a screen reader says the word and skips the symbol,
         and this is the only warning a watch-only user will ever get. -->
    <string name="wear_tile_detail_approx">About %1$s  ·  %2$s</string>
</resources>
